WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.599
Today we're doing the oldest number

00:00:01.599 --> 00:00:03.600
system in the world:

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:07.130
Babylonian Cuneiform Numbers.

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:11.280
( Brady: Cu-ne-iform?)
- Yeah it's true, I thought it was

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:14.480
cuneiform too but then I went I spoke to

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:16.320
one of the curators of the cuneiform

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:17.920
collection at the british museum

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:20.320
and in this first sentence out his mouth

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:22.080
has said cu-ne-iform

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:25.599
so that's how one should say it. 
So the first

00:00:25.599 --> 00:00:28.160
writing system is cuneiform and it

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:30.400
evolved about three thousand years BC

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:32.559
between the Tigris and the Euphrates, the

00:00:32.559 --> 00:00:34.460
kind of Sumerian civilisation.

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:39.840
And what it is it's bits of mud, you make

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:40.640
a mark

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:43.040
using a reed - although here I've got a

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:45.280
lollipop stick and I've got some potty
putty;

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:47.680
but it's essentially the same. Cuneis is

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:49.520
the Latin for wedge and it's all about
making

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:52.640
wedge marks. So when you see it written
down

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:54.160
you see with like little triangles and

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:55.280
lines but that's just because they're

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:57.360
trying to make it look like an actual 3d

00:00:57.360 --> 00:00:58.640
wedge mark, but today

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:00.079
I'm going to be able to show you using

00:01:00.079 --> 00:01:02.239
actual 3d wedge marks.

00:01:02.239 --> 00:01:04.960
So the system of writing things down

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:06.880
emerge around about 3000 BC,

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:08.880
but I'm going to sort of speed forward

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:10.720
to about 2000 BC

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:13.360
when the number system was already kind

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:15.280
of in fully functioning order

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:17.840
and kind of worked, and pretty much the

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:20.479
oldest kind of mathematical tablets that
we have

00:01:20.479 --> 00:01:22.880
are from that period. And it's- you call

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:25.280
that Babylon so it's the kind of
civilisation

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:28.320
that kind of came on after Suma. So how

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:29.600
do Babylonian

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:32.720
numbers work? Well I'm going to take

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:34.960
one of my tablets and- one thing which is

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:36.079
interesting,

00:01:36.079 --> 00:01:39.280
all the tablets that were used to make

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:42.399
cuneiform on are really about this size.

00:01:42.399 --> 00:01:44.000
So often you see them in photographs and

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:45.439
they kind of expanded and you think, well

00:01:45.439 --> 00:01:46.640
maybe this tablet was this kind of

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:48.320
massive tablet like a kind of gravestone,

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:50.079
well no they're all just about this size
almost

00:01:50.079 --> 00:01:52.240
like a- you know say half an iphone or

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:53.600
something.

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:54.880
If you go to the British museum where

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:56.399
they have well over a hundred thousand

00:01:56.399 --> 00:01:57.759
of these tablets - they're more like this,

00:01:57.759 --> 00:01:59.439
they're kind of squareish

00:01:59.439 --> 00:02:00.960
and there's got all the writing on it

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:02.719
because,

00:02:02.719 --> 00:02:04.640
how do you write it? You write it by

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:06.000
holding the tablet in one hand and

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:07.200
holding the reed in the other and then

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:08.080
making the marks.

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:11.920
It was used over a period of 3000 years,

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:14.239
so it's actually the writing system and

00:02:14.239 --> 00:02:15.520
number system that have been used for the

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:17.440
longest period of time,

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:19.840
much more than the Latin alphabet or

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:20.879
Roman numerals

00:02:20.879 --> 00:02:24.239
or Arabic numerals. So in terms of

00:02:24.239 --> 00:02:26.239
say what you call civilisation, since the

00:02:26.239 --> 00:02:28.319
early civilisations it's been used for

00:02:28.319 --> 00:02:30.591
two-thirds really of human history.

00:02:30.591 --> 00:02:32.800
(I wonder if ours will 
ever overtake it or)

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:34.160
(something else will come along?)

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:35.519
(Or I wonder if ours will ever become the)

00:02:35.519 --> 00:02:37.360
(title holder?)

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:40.640
What our Arabic numerals? Well you could

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:44.160
argue that hexadecimal or even base

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:46.319
two has really overtaken it because we

00:02:46.319 --> 00:02:47.680
live in a world of computers and that's

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:49.429
how computers work.

00:02:50.879 --> 00:02:53.599
The number system uses only two types of

00:02:53.599 --> 00:02:54.480
wedge.

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:58.319
1 is this - that's 1.

00:02:58.319 --> 00:03:02.480
2 is another one, 3

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:05.599
that's three. 4, 5,

00:03:05.599 --> 00:03:08.879
6, 7, 8, 9.

00:03:08.879 --> 00:03:11.920
So 1 to 9 you're just adding- it's

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:12.640
quite like

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:14.560
Roman numerals but you're just keeping

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:16.080
on adding the same digits.

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:19.280
When you get to ten you use- I'm

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:20.239
going to turn this over-

00:03:20.239 --> 00:03:24.400
and you use the other sort of horizontal

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:26.799
and use that, so that's 10. 
And you want to

00:03:26.799 --> 00:03:28.560
carry on counting you'd go

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:32.480
back to the vertical...12, 13, 14, 15,

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:35.519
16, 17, 18, 19;

00:03:35.519 --> 00:03:38.200
and 20 would be

00:03:40.959 --> 00:03:43.309
Sorry whoops 
- (Two horizontals?)

00:03:43.309 --> 00:03:45.440
Two horizontals. So keep on going 
they go to

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:47.519
30,

00:03:47.519 --> 00:03:51.440
40, 50;

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:54.720
51, 52, 53,

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:58.879
54, 55, 56, 57, 58, 59 -

00:03:58.879 --> 00:04:01.760
and can you guess what 60 is?
- (Well I)

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:03.120
(would have thought it would be)

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:07.200
(six horizontals?)
- You fell into my trap.

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:09.890
You fell into my trap! This...

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:17.519
is 60. Because you're starting 
off with a system

00:04:17.519 --> 00:04:20.320
which is adding new symbols, which is

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:21.440
just like Roman,

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:23.520
but it also had a positional element. And

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:24.720
when you get to 60

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:26.880
you move along. So this should be a zero

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:27.600
here but the problem with the

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:29.040
Babylonians, they didn't have a symbol

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:30.160
for zero.

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:32.400
Later on they invented a kind of symbol

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:33.199
for zero,

00:04:33.199 --> 00:04:35.520
but actually they never used it in a

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:36.720
case like this;

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:40.720
so that is 60. 61 is this,

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:43.840
62, 63 - actually

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:48.800
that's a better 63 - 64, 65, 66,

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:52.960
67, 68, 69, and 70.

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:56.080
I mean yeah 70 would be

00:04:56.080 --> 00:05:00.080
one 60 and

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:03.440
a 10. It's called a sexagesimal system,

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:05.919
i.e. base 60. You've got this column that

00:05:05.919 --> 00:05:07.199
counts up to 60

00:05:07.199 --> 00:05:08.960
and then this one here, whatever you have

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:11.120
in there you multiply by 60.

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:13.120
So actually just say you wanted to go

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:15.520
further on, that number there

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:22.047
is 3661.

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:27.360
(If you want to do 3671)

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:30.720
(you'd put like a- )
- Yeah that would have

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:33.120
- (A 10 in there?)
- 10 would go here. But if

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:35.280
you wanted to put a 10 here

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:37.039
that would mean you have to times 60 by

00:05:37.039 --> 00:05:39.360
11, which is 660,

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:45.840
so that would be 3600 plus 660

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:49.960
plus 11. 
- (How would you write)

00:05:49.960 --> 00:05:53.520
(3601? Do you have to have like a)

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:54.240
(space)

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:57.360
(in that column?)
- Yes, so that- the

00:05:57.360 --> 00:05:58.720
Babylonians

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:02.000
required a symbol for zero for when

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:03.759
there was nothing in that column.

00:06:03.759 --> 00:06:06.800
And eventually they started to use

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:10.160
two diagonals basically like that, two

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:11.039
diagonals;

00:06:11.039 --> 00:06:14.240
that would be 3601 with the two

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:15.280
diagonals

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:17.440
to indicate nothing in the middle. This

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:19.120
only occurred in later

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:21.840
Babylonian mathematical texts and also

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:23.840
they never used it

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:26.000
at either side, so you couldn't tell the

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:27.840
difference between

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:32.400
sort of 110 and 1100

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:34.479
for example; and just 11.

00:06:34.479 --> 00:06:36.479
But they did use it as a marker to

00:06:36.479 --> 00:06:38.000
separate

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:39.840
middle columns. So you've got this really

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:42.639
interesting combination of a positional
system

00:06:42.639 --> 00:06:45.280
and a sort of non-positional system. And

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:46.319
you might think, well isn't that

00:06:46.319 --> 00:06:47.440
complicated?

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:51.039
It might look complicated, actually

00:06:51.039 --> 00:06:53.199
for doing maths it's really good and

00:06:53.199 --> 00:06:55.280
it's much much better than the 
Roman system.

00:06:55.280 --> 00:06:57.280
With the Roman system, you get up to M,

00:06:57.280 --> 00:06:58.960
that's a thousand. And then if you want

00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:00.080
to get more than a thousand you just

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:03.810
keep on doing Ms, Ms forever.

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:06.080
Whereas here if you want to get bigger

00:07:06.080 --> 00:07:08.000
you just need to go to one new column

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:10.400
and then you get the next multiple of 60

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:11.599
up. So you can actually get

00:07:11.599 --> 00:07:13.759
very quickly to really high numbers; but

00:07:13.759 --> 00:07:15.280
also you can go the other way,

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:17.120
like the decimal point. If you were to

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:18.800
put a 1 here

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:22.000
that would be 160. And if
you were to put a 1

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:25.360
next to that that would be one 3600th.

00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:27.120
It's very good at getting big numbers,

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:28.639
very good at dividing into small numbers,

00:07:28.639 --> 00:07:29.840
much better; because the Romans they use

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:30.960
kind of fractions, it's really

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:31.919
complicated

00:07:31.919 --> 00:07:34.880
and difficult to do whereas this you

00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:35.840
could do very well.

00:07:35.840 --> 00:07:39.199
And it is no coincidence the Babylonians

00:07:39.199 --> 00:07:41.840
were the best astronomers of their age

00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:42.720
by far

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:44.240
and one of the reasons is that they had

00:07:44.240 --> 00:07:46.160
a much much better number system they

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:48.000
could use to do it. And in fact they were

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:49.199
so good

00:07:49.199 --> 00:07:50.879
that the real- you know, you might have

00:07:50.879 --> 00:07:52.879
thought well why 60?

00:07:52.879 --> 00:07:55.599
60 sounds familiar. The reason why - we

00:07:55.599 --> 00:07:57.440
still use base 60 when talking about the

00:07:57.440 --> 00:07:59.680
time, so 60 seconds in a minute,

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:02.800
60 minutes in an hour, is that 60 was

00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:05.280
the number chosen as the base by the

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:07.039
Babylonians right at the beginning.

00:08:07.039 --> 00:08:08.080
And it's because of all their kind of

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:10.319
stargazing etc that that- even though the

00:08:10.319 --> 00:08:11.520
Greeks had a different number
system than the

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:12.400
Romans did,

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:14.960
they just kept the base 60 when talking

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:16.319
about time.

00:08:16.319 --> 00:08:18.319
(And why did the Babylonians choose 60?)

00:08:18.319 --> 00:08:20.000
Well this is really interesting and it's

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:21.759
probably one of the biggest questions in

00:08:21.759 --> 00:08:22.800
ancient mathematics,

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:24.720
no one knows. There are two possible

00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:26.080
explanations; one,

00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:27.599
it could be that- just say you're

00:08:27.599 --> 00:08:29.680
approximating a circle,

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:31.599
you might want to put a hexagon in it.

00:08:31.599 --> 00:08:33.279
A hexagon is actually

00:08:33.279 --> 00:08:37.120
six triangles with 60 
degrees, so already -

00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:39.360
just say you want to divide each sixth

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:40.159
into ten -

00:08:40.159 --> 00:08:42.479
then you get sixty. So that's one reason

00:08:42.479 --> 00:08:44.399
they think-
- (But why divide it into ten?)

00:08:44.399 --> 00:08:47.040
(Are we coming back to fingers now?)

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:49.360
Yes because the thing that's most

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:51.040
natural to do,

00:08:51.040 --> 00:08:53.920
in the way to count, and there was a base

00:08:53.920 --> 00:08:55.120
10 system

00:08:55.120 --> 00:08:58.320
embedded in their base 60 system, because
we get

00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:00.080
just the horizontal lines until we get

00:09:00.080 --> 00:09:02.160
to 10 when we come to this other

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:05.120
symbol, is that here we've got the sixes;

00:09:05.120 --> 00:09:06.160
the most

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:07.920
natural thing to divide a circle to is

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:10.880
into sixths. You know, we got to 360
degrees,

00:09:10.880 --> 00:09:12.320
so that's interesting already, we've kept

00:09:12.320 --> 00:09:14.080
that 360 degrees.

00:09:14.080 --> 00:09:15.680
Divided into six so that each one is 60

00:09:15.680 --> 00:09:17.440
degrees then maybe it's easier just to

00:09:17.440 --> 00:09:18.720
then use your hands and divide that

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:21.360
into 10. The other reason why 60
is really good

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:24.080
is that 60 is a very divisible number.

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:26.240
The numbers that divide into 60 are 1, 2,

00:09:26.240 --> 00:09:30.000
3, 4, 5, 6,

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:33.839
10, 12, 20, 30 and then 60.

00:09:33.839 --> 00:09:35.839
That's really good for doing arithmetic

00:09:35.839 --> 00:09:37.440
in your head and also

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:39.279
for just dividing stuff up; it's like-

00:09:39.279 --> 00:09:40.880
it's like really really useful.

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:42.560
So that- it could be that, it could be a

00:09:42.560 --> 00:09:45.120
mixture of both. I mean some people think
that;

00:09:45.120 --> 00:09:47.519
60 is also 5 times 12 they could have

00:09:47.519 --> 00:09:48.399
been a

00:09:48.399 --> 00:09:51.680
base 5 number system, because

00:09:51.680 --> 00:09:54.320
ten, that's one system; they are also base

00:09:54.320 --> 00:09:55.600
side number systems with the five

00:09:55.600 --> 00:09:56.560
figures on our hand,

00:09:56.560 --> 00:09:58.160
and they could have been a base 12.

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:00.800
12 is also a very divisible number.

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:03.920
No one quite knows. But it's interesting

00:10:03.920 --> 00:10:05.920
that the very oldest type of number

00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:08.800
is still present with us every day.

